---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import CategoryHub from '../../components/CategoryHub.astro';
import AffiliateDisclosure from '../../components/AffiliateDisclosure.astro';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Wealth screening & prospect research software for nonprofits"
  description="DonorSearch, Kindsight (iWave), and WealthEngine compared: what wealth screening costs, what it tells you, and the shop size where it pays off."
  section="prospect-research">
  <div class="wrap">
    <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ label: 'Home', href: '/' }, { label: 'Wealth prospecting' }]} />
    <section class="prose" style="margin-top: 1.2rem;">
      <p class="kicker">Category guide</p>
      <h1>Wealth screening &amp; prospect research</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Wealth screening scores your donors on capacity (assets, real estate,
        giving history) so gift officers spend cultivation time on the right
        people. It's the most expensive category in the stack and the easiest to
        buy too early.
      </p>

      <AffiliateDisclosure />
    </section>

    <CategoryHub category="prospect-research" part="table" />

    <section class="prose" style="max-width: none;">


      <h2>How to choose</h2>
      <p>
        Pricing is quoted per organization across the category; reported entry
        contracts run about $4,000-$6,000 a year.
        <a href="/go/donorsearch" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener">DonorSearch</a>
        leads with demonstrated giving history.
        <a href="/go/kindsight" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener">Kindsight</a>
        (formerly iWave) posts the category's top satisfaction scores with
        transparent, sourced ratings.
        <a href="/go/wealthengine" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener">WealthEngine</a>
        specializes in fast capacity modeling across large lists. The best
        evaluation is the same for all three: book the demos and ask each vendor
        to score a sample of donors you already know well.
      </p>
      <p>
        The timing rule: screening pays off when a named person works the results
        into portfolios and visits, typically around $1M raised. Before that,
        hand-research your top 25 prospects; when that stops keeping up, you're
        ready to buy. And remember what the data is: capacity says someone could
        give, and cultivation discovers whether they will.
      </p>
    </section>

    <CategoryHub category="prospect-research" part="tools" />

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Screening enters the recommended stack at
        <a href="/stacks/1m-5m/">$1M-$5M</a> and runs continuously at
        <a href="/stacks/5m-plus/">$5M+</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
